!===========================================================
!  CMFGEN VADAT for SN model
!  Comments summarise behaviour and recommended usage.
!===========================================================


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 1. Atmospheric structure & basic SN setup
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Inner radius of the model in units of 1e10 cm.
! For SN models this is the inner boundary of the ejecta grid
! (not the radius at Rosseland tau=2/3 as for hydrostatic stars).
! It must correspond to the innermost radius in SN_HYDRO_DATA.
0.32161021      [RSTAR]

! Outer radius in units of the inner radius.
! For SN models this should match the ratio R_outer / R_inner
! implied by SN_HYDRO_DATA. Very inconsistent values can cause
! failures in velocity-law and grid setup.
2.16178924D+07  [RMAX]

! Velocity law selector.
! Value 11: use the velocity and density directly from the
! SN hydrodynamic structure (SN_HYDRO_DATA).
11              [VEL_LAW]

! Terminal velocity in km/s, used as a reference scale.
! For a hydrodynamic SN model it should be broadly consistent
! with the maximum velocity in SN_HYDRO_DATA.
80472.26        [VINF]

! Total luminosity in units of L_sun.
! For hydrodynamic SN models the emergent luminosity is largely
! set by the hydrodynamics and radioactive heating; this field
! is often used only for bookkeeping.
0.0             [LSTAR]

! Total mass in units of M_sun.
! Should be consistent with the ejecta mass used in the hydro
! simulation; used in some diagnostic output.
9.28497         [MASS]

! Global clumping switch.
! T: use a depth-dependent volume filling factor f(r).
! F: treat the ejecta as smooth and unclumped.
! SN models are often started with clumping switched off.
F               [DO_CL]

! Choice of clumping law, only used when clumping is enabled.
! NONE indicates that no analytic clumping law is used.
NONE            [CL_LAW]

! Number of free clumping parameters for the chosen law.
! Must be consistent with the selected clumping prescription.
0               [N_CL_PAR]



! Sequence number in an SN model time series (1,2,3,...).
! Useful for tracking models in a time sequence; not physical.
1               [TS_NO]

! Age of the explosion in days.
! Must match the age in SN_HYDRO_DATA so that any time-dependent
! terms and decay power are consistent.
2.5925974E+01   [SN_AGE]

! Option controlling how the initial temperature structure is obtained.
! Using the hydro temperature is recommended for the first model.
USE_HYDRO       [SN_T_OPT]

! When true, enforce a strictly homologous flow (velocity ∝ radius).
! When false, preserve the exact velocity profile from the hydro input,
! which is preferred if the hydro solution is not strictly homologous.
F               [PURE_HUB]

! Treatment of gamma-ray energy from radioactive decays.
! LOCAL deposits all decay energy locally without explicit transport.
! Other choices allow approximate or externally computed transport.
LOCAL           [GAMRAY_TRANS]

! Non-thermal electron solver switch.
! When true, CMFGEN solves for the non-thermal electron distribution
! and includes non-thermal ionisation and excitation explicitly.
! At early times this can often be neglected; at nebular epochs it
! becomes important.
F               [TRT_NON_TE]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 2. Time-dependence, radioactive decays, adiabatic terms
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Local flag documenting that adiabatic effects are considered.
! Actual inclusion of adiabatic cooling is controlled by other
! dedicated switches.
T               [INCL_ADIABATIC]

! Include adiabatic cooling in the energy equation.
! This term is important when the expansion timescale is short
! compared to radiative or thermal timescales, as in SN ejecta.
! It can be turned off temporarily for debugging.
F [INC_AD]

! Include advection (flow) terms in the rate equations.
! In SN modelling, explicit time dependence is usually controlled
! via the comoving time-derivative; advection is an additional
! correction that may be switched on for realism once basic
! convergence is established.
F [INC_ADV]

! Include the comoving time derivative D/Dt in the statistical
! equilibrium equations.
! This is needed for fully time-dependent SN sequences; for a purely
! stationary snapshot this is usually left false.
F               [DO_DDT]

! Include the energy input from radioactive decays.
! Essential for SN models whose luminosity is powered by decaying
! isotopes such as nickel and cobalt.
F               [INC_RAD_DECAYS]

! Local diagnostic flag related to Ng usage; does not affect physics.
F               [CHK_NG]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 3. Continuum frequency grid
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Read a precomputed continuum frequency set from file.
! When false, the continuum grid is constructed internally.
F               [RD_CF_FILE]

! Minimum continuum frequency in units of 10^15 Hz.
! Sets the low-frequency limit of the transfer grid.
0.00149897      [MIN_CF]

! Maximum continuum frequency in units of 10^15 Hz.
! Must be high enough to include all important bound-free edges,
! and extended further if X-rays are present.
50.000D0        [MAX_CF]

! Spacing factor for low frequencies.
! Frequencies are geometrically spaced with this factor below a
! certain threshold; values close to unity give finer spacing.
1.10D0          [FRAC_SP]

! Spacing factor for high frequencies.
! Sets the geometric factor for stepping to higher frequencies.
1.05D0          [AMP_FAC]

! Maximum spacing near bound-free edges (in 10^15 Hz).
! Controls how finely edges are sampled for recombination rates.
0.10D0          [MAX_BF]

! Enable level dissolution in the treatment of bound-free opacities.
! This improves behaviour near series limits by turning dense high-n
! lines into a pseudo-continuum.
T               [DO_DIS]

! Velocity spacing in km/s used on the low-frequency side of a
! bound-free edge when inserting extra continuum frequencies.
200.0D0         [dV_LEV]

! Factor by which the spacing grows as one moves away from the
! bound-free edge; expressed relative to the base spacing.
1.4             [AMP_DIS]

! Lower frequency bound (in 10^15 Hz) for applying the special
! dissolution spacing rules.
0.1             [MIN_DIS]

! Control of where the continuum opacity and emissivity are evaluated.
! When false, chi and eta are evaluated only at a subset of frequencies
! separated by the chosen velocity spacing and interpolated in between,
! which speeds up the calculation.
F               [CROSS]

! Maximum velocity separation in km/s between frequencies at which
! the continuum opacity and emissivity are explicitly computed when
! interpolation is used.
750.0           [V_CROSS]

! Gaussian width in km/s used to smooth photoionisation cross-sections.
! If the atomic data are already smoothed, CMFGEN will avoid excessive
! additional smoothing.
750.0           [SIG_GAU_KMS]

! Extent, in units of the terminal velocity, over which a line is
! allowed to influence the continuum and rates beyond its resonance
! zone in the comoving frame.
0.5             [EXT_LINE_VAR]

! Threshold for identifying lines whose net radiative bracket is
! sufficiently close to zero that a net-rate linearisation can be used.
! When the net bracket is within this fractional tolerance of unity,
! the line behaves effectively optically thin.
0.01            [ZNET_VAR_LIM]

! Choose whether weak lines are treated using net rates.
! When true and the line satisfies the weakness criteria, full
! linearisation can be avoided for that transition to save time.
T               [WNET]

! Weak-line criterion based on the maximum ratio of line opacity
! to continuum opacity.
! When this ratio stays below the chosen limit, the line is considered
! weak for the purposes of net-rate treatment.
0.10            [WK_LIM]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 4. Radiation-field solver & relativity
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Use the fully relativistic moment solver for the radiation field.
! This includes all v/c terms in the comoving-frame moment equations,
! which is important for fast SN ejecta.
T               [USE_J_REL]

! Include relativistic corrections in the comoving-frame transfer
! equation.
T               [INCL_REL]

! Include advection terms in the comoving-frame transfer equation,
! adding further v/c corrections to the transport.
T               [INCL_ADV_TRANS]

! Include the time derivative of J in the transfer equation.
! For strictly stationary SN models this is disabled; for explicit
! time-dependent transport this is switched on.
F               [INCL_DJDT]

! Force use of the transfer routines that include the J time derivative,
! even if the derivative itself is not applied.
F               [USE_DJDT_RTE]

! Compute the continuum mean intensity J using Eddington factors
! derived from a separate formal solution.
T               [JC_W_EDD]

! Similarly, compute the line pseudo-continuum mean intensity using
! Eddington factors.
T               [JBAR_W_EDD]

! Use the diffusion approximation at the inner boundary.
! This assumes the innermost layers are optically thick and in
! radiative equilibrium.
T               [DIF]

! Explicit choice of the inner boundary method as diffusion.
DIFFUSION       [IB_METH]

! Treat electron scattering as coherent in frequency in the CMF solver.
! For more realistic scattering in flux calculations, incoherent
! scattering is typically handled in CMF_FLUX.
T               [COH_ES]

! Historical flag controlling alternate use of previously computed J;
! kept only for backward compatibility.
F               [OLD_J]

! When true, use a mixture of coherent and incoherent scattering in
! the linearisation based on the similarity between J and the
! scattered J; still considered experimental.
F               [MIX_COH]

! Threshold on the relative difference between the mean intensity and
! the electron-scattered mean intensity when deciding whether to use
! coherent behaviour in the variation of J.
0.1             [ES_FAC]

! Choice of closure relation for the highest moment in the moment
! equations.
! Here, the ratio N/J is used, which is more stable than N/H when
! H passes through zero at some radii.
N_ON_J          [N_TYPE]

! Method used to solve the transfer equation along rays.
! The integral short-characteristics method is used, with additional
! insertion of points near the tangential plane to improve accuracy.
INT/INS         [FG_OPT]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 5. Accuracy options for chi, tau, moments
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Method for computing optical depth from opacity.
! The chosen method uses trapezoidal integration with monotonic
! derivatives in log-log space to avoid spurious oscillations.
LOGMON          [METHOD]

! Maximum allowed change in velocity per step for the ray-based
! computation of Eddington factors.
! Large values effectively disable the automatic insertion of
! extra points along rays.
20000.0D0       [VFRAC_FG]

! Maximum velocity step for the moment-based solution grid.
! Large values also disable extra point insertion in that stage.
20000.0D0       [VFRAC_MOM]

! Use the thick-boundary treatment for the continuum at the outer
! boundary. This improves the behaviour of the flux and moments in
! the outermost layers.
T               [THK_CONT]

! Use trapezoidal weighting of intensities when computing the angle
! moments J, H, K, N from the discrete-ray intensities.
T               [TRAP_J]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 6. Line broadening & intrinsic profiles
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Reference temperature (in 10^4 K) for computing thermal Doppler
! widths of spectral lines, when a fixed temperature is assumed.
4.0D0           [TDOP]

! Atomic mass (in atomic mass units) used in the Doppler-width
! calculation when a single representative ion is assumed.
4.0D0           [AMASS_DOP]

! Microturbulent velocity (km/s) used in computing the intrinsic
! line profile widths.
50.0D0          [VTURB]

! Half-width of the resonance zone, in units of the Doppler width,
! used in the comoving-frame line radiative transfer.
6.0             [MAX_DOP]

! Frequency spacing across the resonance zone, expressed in units of
! the Doppler width, for sampling the intrinsic line profile.
1.0D0           [FRAC_DOP]

! Velocity spacing (km/s) between sample points across the full
! comoving-frame line profile outside the resonance zone.
200.0           [dV_CMF_PROF]

! Velocity spacing (km/s) for sampling the non-coherent electron
! scattering wings in the comoving frame.
300.0           [dV_CMF_WING]

! Extent of the non-coherent electron scattering wings, in km/s,
! beyond the resonance zone in frequency space.
2500.0          [ES_WING_EXT]

! Extent, in units of the terminal velocity, of the coherent electron
! scattering wings beyond the resonance zone.
3.0             [R_CMF_WING_EXT]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 7. Observed-frame spectrum control (within CMFGEN)
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Half-width of the observed-frame line profile in units of the
! terminal velocity. Determines how far the observed spectrum is
! computed about the line centre.
1.1             [OBS_EXT_RAT]

! Velocity spacing (km/s) of the observed-frame spectrum across
! the central part of the line.
100.0           [dV_OBS_PROF]

! Velocity spacing (km/s) in the observed-frame electron scattering
! wings.
400.0           [dV_OBS_WING]

! Velocity spacing (km/s) between lines (in continuum regions) in
! the observed-frame spectrum.
2000.0          [dV_OBS_BIG]

! When true, the run is a pure flux calculation with no update of
! populations; here it is left false since CMFGEN is used for the
! structure.
F               [FLUX_CAL_ONLY]

! When true, the formal solution is extended outward in radius by an
! additional factor to test sensitivity to the outer boundary; here
! this feature is disabled.
F               [EXT_FRM_SOL]

! When true, extra frequencies are inserted in the formal solution to
! improve the resolution of line profiles; generally recommended.
T               [INS_F_FRM_SOL]

! Choice of observed-frame formal solver; here the integral
! short-characteristics method is used.
INT             [FRM_OPT]

! When true, equivalent widths of lines are computed using the
! Sobolev approximation during the run; left false here.
F               [DO_SOB_LINES]

! When true, the frequencies associated with Sobolev/CMF lines are
! included in defining the observed-frame grid; left false here.
F               [SOB_FREQ_IN_OBS]

! Controls which wavelengths are treated with the global line handling
! mode vs. pure continuum; when true, the global mode applies between
! the specified lower and upper limits.
T               [LAM_SET]

! Lower wavelength limit (in Angstrom) for including lines in the
! flux calculation.
5.0E+01         [F_LAM_BEG]

! Upper wavelength limit (in Angstrom) for including lines in the
! flux calculation.
5.0E+04         [F_LAM_END]


!---------- SN outer/inner fine-grid control ----------
[N_IB_INS]  4      ! number of points for fine grid at inner boundary (default is 2)
[N_OB_INS]  8      ! number of points for fine grid at outer boundary (default is 3)
[REV_RGRID]  T     ! allow automatic revision of the R grid (primarily for SN models)

!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 8. Treatment of bound-bound transitions
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Global choice of line treatment for all species.
! BLANK uses full line blanketing with overlapping lines treated
! explicitly in the comoving frame.
BLANK           [GLOBAL_LINE]

! Threshold on gf for omitting very weak lines, in combination with
! a lower-level index requirement and atomic-number cutoff.
1.0D-3          [GF_CUT]

! Only transitions with lower-level indices above this value are
! considered for the gf-cut, so that important low-lying transitions
! are always retained.
9               [GF_LEV_CUT]

! Atomic-number threshold above which the gf-cut is applied.
! This allows gf-filtering to be restricted mostly to iron-group
! and heavier elements.
22              [AT_CUT]

! Minimum number of downward transitions that a level must have
! before any of its lines can be omitted, ensuring that no level
! is completely disconnected.
10              [MIN_TRANS]

! Use a thick-line boundary condition in the CMF solver for lines,
! ensuring consistent treatment of line source functions at the
! outer boundary.
T               [THK_LINE]

! Adjust negative line opacities arising from population inversions
! so that the total opacity remains positive and numerically stable.
T               [CHK_L_POS]

! Strategy for handling negative line opacities in the blanketed
! calculation. The chosen option ties the opacity floor to a
! fraction of the electron scattering opacity.
ESEC_CHK        [NEG_OPAC_OPT]

! When true, sets the rates in He II resonance lines to zero.
! This is only for specialised tests; here it is left false.
F               [He2_RES=0]

! Allow Sobolev overlap between different lines when the Sobolev
! approximation is used; not relevant in fully blanketed CMF mode.
F               [ALLOW_OL]

! Maximum velocity separation (km/s) between overlapping Sobolev
! resonance zones for treating them as a coupled system.
50.0            [OL_DIF]

! Apply a scaling correction to the line heating and cooling rates
! to compensate for errors introduced by grouping many levels into
! super-levels.
T               [SCL_LN]

! Restrict the application of the line heating/cooling scaling to
! cases where line frequencies are within a certain fractional
! spacing of each other.
0.5             [SCL_LN_FAC]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 9. Physical process switches
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Include charge exchange reactions with hydrogen and helium.
! For many hot, highly ionised SN conditions, this can be left off;
! it is more relevant when significant neutral fractions are present.
F               [INC_CHG]

! Include two-photon decay processes (e.g. 2s->1s in hydrogen).
! This can usually be turned on without harm, but is off here.
F               [INC_TWO]

! Include Penning ionisation processes.
! Rarely used in stellar and SN modelling; left off.
F               [INC_PEN]

! Include Rayleigh scattering by neutral hydrogen.
! Important primarily in the far-UV; off here for simplicity.
F               [INC_RAY]

! Include additional X-ray emission from shocks in the wind or
! ejecta; off here as no shock-generated X-ray component is used.
F               [INC_XRAYS]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 10. Starting a new model (initialisation)
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Read a custom radial grid from a file analogous to the departure
! coefficient files; used mainly for specialised tests.
F               [RD_IN_R_GRID]

! Use linear interpolation from a previous model’s populations
! rather than starting from separate XzV IN files.
! For first SN models or large parameter jumps this is usually
! left false.
F               [LIN_INT]

! Renormalise the populations to satisfy abundance constraints
! exactly during initialisation; typically not needed.
F               [POP_SCALE]

! Perform a separate initial iteration on the temperature structure
! before the main run. For SN models using a good hydro temperature
! guess this can be left false.
F               [IT_ON_T]

! Electron-scattering or Rosseland optical depth above which the
! initial temperature is re-estimated from a simple thermal balance
! or grey-solution approach.
20.0            [T_INIT_TAU]

! Rosseland optical depth above which the temperature is forced to
! follow a grey-atmosphere solution during initialisation.
10.0            [GREY_TAU]

! Use LTE populations for the initial guess.
! For SN models this is often combined with a minimum excitation
! temperature to avoid underflows in low-density outer layers.
F               [LTE_EST]

! Minimum excitation temperature in units of 10^4 K used when
! computing initial LTE populations. Prevents extremely low
! temperatures from producing unphysical population ratios.
0.6             [T_EXC]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 11. Species-specific line options & dielectronic recombination (global)
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Global line-treatment mode (BLANK) already set above for most species.
! The following per-ion options override that global choice for selected ions.

!----------------------------------------
! Per-ion line treatment switches (CMF / SOB)
!----------------------------------------

! Treat neutral hydrogen lines with the CMF (non-Sobolev, non-blanketed)
! line transfer solver for testing and comparison. This overrides the
! global BLANK mode for this ion.
CMF          [TRANS_HI]

! Treat neutral He I lines with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_HeI]

! Treat singly ionised He II lines with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_He2]

! Treat neutral carbon lines (C I) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_CI]

! Treat singly ionised carbon lines (C II) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_C2]

! Treat doubly ionised carbon lines (C III) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_CIII]

! Treat triply ionised carbon lines (C IV) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_CIV]

! Treat neutral nitrogen lines (N I) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_NI]

! Treat singly ionised nitrogen lines (N II) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_N2]

! Treat doubly ionised nitrogen lines (N III) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_NIII]

! Treat triply ionised nitrogen lines (N IV) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_NIV]

! Treat quadruply ionised nitrogen lines (N V) with the CMF solver.
CMF          [TRANS_NV]

! Treat neutral oxygen lines (O I) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_OI]

! Treat singly ionised oxygen lines (O II) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_O2]

! Treat doubly ionised oxygen lines (O III) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_OIII]

! Treat triply ionised oxygen lines (O IV) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_OIV]

! Treat quadruply ionised oxygen lines (O V) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_OV]

! Treat highly ionised oxygen lines (O VI/OSIX) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_OSIX]

! Treat singly ionised silicon lines (Sk2 ≡ Si II) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_Sk2]

! Treat doubly ionised silicon lines (SkIII ≡ Si III) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_SkIII]

! Treat triply ionised silicon lines (SkIV ≡ Si IV) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_SkIV]

! Treat doubly ionised sulphur lines (S III) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_SIII]

! Treat triply ionised sulphur lines (S IV) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_SIV]

! Treat quadruply ionised sulphur lines (S V) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_SV]

! Treat highly ionised sulphur lines (S VI/SSIX) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_SSIX]

! Treat doubly ionised iron lines (Fe III) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_FeIII]

! Treat triply ionised iron lines (Fe IV) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_FeIV]

! Treat quadruply ionised iron lines (Fe V) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_FeV]

! Treat highly ionised iron lines (Fe VI/FeSIX) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_FeSIX]

! Treat even higher iron ionisation stages (Fe VII/FeSEV) with the Sobolev approximation.
SOB          [TRANS_FeSEV]


!----------------------------------------
! Additional element abundances written as negative values so
! they are interpreted as mass fractions. Together with the
! entries above, these should sum to unity.
!----------------------------------------

! Abundance of hydrogen, written as a negative value so that it
! is interpreted as a mass fraction. The set of negative entries
! for all elements should sum to unity (to numerical precision).

-5.207197D-02   [HYD/X]
-1.07D-01       [HE/X]
-4.23D-01       [OXY/X]
-1.21D-02       [CAL/X]
-4.6322869D-02  [CARB/X]
-5.9004106D-05  [NIT/X]
-1.1656253D-01  [NEON/X]
-4.7441808D-02  [MAG/X]
-1.6312100D-01  [SIL/X]
-3.6666922D-03  [IRON/X]
-0.0D-6         [PHOS/X]
-0.0D-06        [SUL/X]
-2.8189559D-02  [NICK/X]
-1.1194512D-02  [ARG/X]
-1.2234476D-04  [TIT/X]
-1.2256985D-03  [CHRO/X]


!----------------------------------------
! Detailed dielectronic recombination switches
!----------------------------------------

! Treat low-temperature dielectronic recombination as separate,
! non-overlapping lines (T) or subsume into photoionisation
! cross-sections (F). Here left false globally; per-ion flags below
! control which LTDR sets are used.
F            [DIE_AS_LINE]

! Velocity scale (km/s) for smoothing dielectronic recombination
! features merged into bound-free cross-sections or treated as lines.
1000.0D0     [VSM_DIE]


! Per-ion LTDR inclusion flags: first logical controls standard LS-allowed
! autoionising states, second logical controls LS-forbidden or special
! states. They determine whether tabulated Nussbaumer–Storey / WI data
! are used for each ion.

F,F          [DIE_HI]
F,F          [DIE_HeI]
F,F          [DIE_He2]

F,F          [DIE_CI]
F,F          [DIE_C2]
F,T          [DIE_CIII]
F,F          [DIE_CIV]
F,F          [DIE_CaI]
F,F          [DIE_Ca2]
F,F          [DIE_CaIII]
F,F          [DIE_NI]
F,F          [DIE_N2]
F,F          [DIE_NIII]
F,T          [DIE_NIV]
F,F          [DIE_NV]

F,F          [DIE_OI]
F,F          [DIE_O2]
F,F          [DIE_OIII]
F,F          [DIE_OIV]
T,T          [DIE_OV]
F,F          [DIE_OSIX]

F,F          [DIE_Sk2]
F,F          [DIE_SkIII]
F,F          [DIE_SkIV]

F,F          [DIE_PIII]
F,F          [DIE_PIV]
F,F          [DIE_PV]
F,F          [DIE_PPIX]

F,F          [DIE_SIII]
F,F          [DIE_SIV]
F,F          [DIE_SV]
F,F          [DIE_SSIX]

F,F          [DIE_FeIII]
F,F          [DIE_FeIV]
F,F          [DIE_FeV]
F,F          [DIE_FeSIX]
F,F          [DIE_FeSEV]


F,F          [DIE_NkIII]

!----------------------------------------
! Level-fixing controls for convergence
!----------------------------------------

! For each ion below, the integer gives the number of lowest levels
! that are to be held fixed during iterations (typically 0, meaning
! all levels are free to respond).

0            [FIX_HI]
0            [FIX_HYD]
0            [FIX_HeI]
0            [FIX_He2]
0            [FIX_HE]

0            [FIX_CI]
0            [FIX_C2]
0            [FIX_CIII]
0            [FIX_CIV]
0            [FIX_CARB]

0            [FIX_NI]
0            [FIX_N2]
0            [FIX_NIII]
0            [FIX_NIV]
0            [FIX_NV]
0            [FIX_NIT]

0            [FIX_OI]
0            [FIX_O2]
0            [FIX_OIII]
0            [FIX_OIV]
0            [FIX_OV]
0            [FIX_OSIX]
0            [FIX_OXY]

0            [FIX_Sk2]
0            [FIX_SkIII]
0            [FIX_SkIV]
0            [FIX_SIL]

0            [FIX_SIII]
0            [FIX_SIV]
0            [FIX_SV]
0            [FIX_SSIX]
0            [FIX_SUL]

0            [FIX_FeIII]
0            [FIX_FeIV]
0            [FIX_FeV]
0            [FIX_FeSIX]
0            [FIX_FeSEV]
0            [FIX_IRON]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 12. Convergence aids: T / NE / impurities
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Fix the electron density during iterations.
! When false, the electron density is solved consistently with
! ionisation balance.
F               [FIX_NE]

! Automatically hold impurity species fixed during iterations when
! their populations are extremely small and may cause noise in the
! convergence.
F               [FIX_IMP]

! Hold the temperature fixed during iterations.
! When true, CMFGEN does not update T, which can help stabilise
! early iterations; here it is currently false so T is free.
T [FIX_T]

! Automatically release the temperature to be solved once other
! corrections fall below a specified threshold.
! Left false here to keep manual control over FIX_T.
F               [FIX_T_AUTO]

! Fix the temperature at the inner boundary over a chosen number
! of depths. Used only in specialised SN runs with very large depth
! optical depths.
F               [FIX_INB_T]

! Legacy option for fixing the temperature above a specified
! Rosseland optical depth; superseded by more flexible controls.
0.0             [TAU_SCL_T]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 13. Linearisation / BA matrix / iteration control
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Choice of linearisation matrix structure across depths.
! A tridiagonal structure couples each depth to its immediate
! neighbours and gives good convergence while keeping memory
! manageable.
TRIDIAG         [SOL_METH]

! Method used to scale corrections before applying them.
! In this mode the largest correction at each depth is limited
! to a chosen factor, while weaker corrections are scaled down
! accordingly; impurity species are handled separately.
MAJOR           [SCALE_OPT]

! Convergence criterion for the largest fractional correction
! (in percent). When the maximum correction falls below this
! value on a full linearisation, the run is considered converged.
1.0D-02 [EPS_TERM]

! Maximum allowed multiplicative change to any variable in
! a single full linearisation iteration.
10.0D0          [MAX_LIN]

! Maximum allowed multiplicative change during a Lambda
! iteration (used when linearisation is temporarily suspended).
1.0D+05         [MAX_LAM]

! Hard limit on how large any correction is allowed to become;
! larger corrections cause the run to stop to avoid numerical
! instabilities.
1.0D+40         [MAX_CHNG]

! Always recompute the BA matrix on iterations that are designated
! full linearisation steps.
F               [COMP_BA]

! Store the inverse of the BA matrix in scratch files to
! accelerate subsequent solves when BA is held fixed.
T               [STORE_BA_INV]

! Write part of the BA inverse to disk in order to reduce
! main-memory usage during the run.
T               [WR_PRT_INV]

! Number of successive iterations for which the BA matrix can
! be held fixed once corrections become small.
2               [N_FIX_BA]

! Store the BA matrix itself to disk (BAMAT) for reuse
! on subsequent iterations.
T               [STORE_BA]

! When the fractional change in J falls below this threshold,
! variations in chi and eta may be ignored and the BA matrix
! held fixed to save time.
1.0D-04         [BA_CHK_FAC]

! Threshold on the maximum correction (in percent) below which
! BA recomputation is turned off and the stored matrix is reused.
15.0D0          [FIX_BA]

! If the largest correction exceeds this factor, the iteration
! type is switched to a pure Lambda iteration for stability.
400.0D0         [LAM_VAL]

! Maximum number of Lambda iterations permitted between
! full linearisation iterations.
2               [NUM_LAM]

! Read an explicitly supplied solution vector to start an
! iteration; disabled here so the run starts from the usual
! initial conditions.
F               [RD_SOL]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 14. Enhanced spatial grid & Eddington factors
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Enable the use of an enhanced spatial grid for solving the
! transfer equation, with additional points inserted between
! depths. Off here for simplicity.
T               [INC_GRID]

! When the enhanced grid is used, choose whether to apply it
! at all frequencies or only above a chosen threshold.
F               [ALL_FREQ]

! Frequency in units of 10^15 Hz below which the enhanced grid
! is not applied.
3.19            [ACC_END]

! Number of intermediate points inserted between each pair
! of original depth points when the enhanced grid is active.
2               [N_INS]

! Index of the first depth at which extra points are inserted
! for the enhanced grid.
1               [ST_INT]

! Index of the last depth for which the enhanced grid is used.
30              [END_INT]

! Number of depths counted inwards from the outer boundary for
! which a quadratic interpolation is used to preserve the
! diffusion approximation in the deep layers.
50              [ND_QUAD]

! Interpolation scheme used when defining the enhanced spatial
! grid: logarithmic spacing is chosen here.
LOG             [INTERP_TYPE]

! Frequency interval, expressed as the number of frequencies,
! after which contributions to the BA matrix from the current
! frequency are accumulated into the stored BA matrix.
200             [N_PAR]

! Whether to compute new Eddington factors on the first
! iteration of a run.
T               [COMP_F]

! Target accuracy for the computation of Eddington factors
! in the moment solver, in fractional units.
1.0D-04         [ACC_F]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 15. Ng acceleration
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Enable Ng acceleration, which extrapolates successive iterations
! to accelerate convergence when corrections have become small.
F [DO_NG]

! Threshold on the maximum correction (in percent) below which
! Ng acceleration is allowed to operate.
5.0D0           [BEG_NG]

! Earliest iteration on which Ng acceleration is allowed,
! so that the outer layers have responded to changes at depth
! before extrapolation is attempted.
15              [IBEG_NG]

! Depth bandwidth (in number of depth points) over which Ng
! acceleration is applied in a single block.
60              [BW_NG]

! Number of iterations between successive Ng accelerations.
8               [ITS/NG]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! 16. Relativistic observer-frame options
!-----------------------------------------------------------

! Include all relativistic terms when computing the observed
! spectrum from the comoving-frame solution.
T               [REL_OBS]

! Include all relativistic terms when computing the comoving
! intensities that are later transformed to the observer frame.
T               [REL_CMF]


!-----------------------------------------------------------
! DEBUG STEPS
!-----------------------------------------------------------


F  [AUTO_ADD]
